agent-power-manager (0.0.3) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Add `apm upgrade`: reconcile every installed power against one catalog
    snapshot and upgrade them all. It takes no positional argument -- single
    resource upgrades stay on `apm install <power>[=<version>]` -- and
    defaults to every installed agent, ordered by agent ID.
  * Upgrade never downgrades. When the catalog latest is behind the local
    version the entry is skipped with a warning (skip=newer-local), and there
    is no --allow-downgrade. An entry whose name has no catalog candidate at
    all is skipped as not-in-catalog instead of failing the whole run.
  * A single failure is reported as `failed` and the remaining entries keep
    going, unlike install's fail-fast. The one condition that does stop the
    run is a residual transaction journal, because a next transaction cannot
    be created safely; the rest is reported as `unexecuted` with a hint to
    run `apm fix`.
  * The outcome vocabulary is deliberately separate from install's:
    installed/upgraded/downgraded/planned/up-to-date/skipped/failed/
    unexecuted. `unchanged` could not be reused because it conflates "the
    explicit request is already satisfied" with "the plan decided to do
    nothing", and `planned` keeps --dry-run from claiming a change happened.
    Install results are mapped faithfully, so a repair after a manually
    deleted target reports `installed` rather than posing as `upgraded`.
  * MCP replacement needs the previous version's catalog entry for rollback,
    so a withdrawn local MCP version is skipped; the other three types do not
    depend on it and still upgrade. --dry-run writes nothing at all, and an
    empty reconciliation (nothing installed) returns before any catalog
    access, so it needs no network.
  * --json is a machine contract: records always carry agent, type, name,
    current_version, target_version, outcome and skip, while warnings and
    skip reasons stay on stderr. See ADR-045 and Feature 032.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:20:00 +0800


agent-power-manager (0.0.2) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Rename the Expert power type locally. APC still calls it "agent" on the wire
    (metadata type, catalog group, content path), and the disk layout keeps
    agents/<name> and agent.yaml, so all of that is translated at the HTTP
    boundary in internal/cloud/remote/type_vocabulary.go instead of being
    renamed out from under APC, Bibit and already-published content.
  * The CLI value is now --type=expert. --type=agent is still accepted and is
    rewritten to expert on the spot, with a deprecation note on stderr unless
    --json was requested: that mode's stdout is a machine contract.
  * The rest of the vocabulary follows: Go symbols and file names that denote
    the power *category* became Expert, while everything that denotes the target
    agent (--agent, AgentID, AgentAdapter) or the author-facing artifact
    (agents/, agent.yaml, AgentConfig) keeps its name. The keep list is written
    down in ADR-044 so the split is a decision rather than an accident.
  * Must be installed together with bibit-agenthub-plugin 0.4.0: an older
    plugin cannot parse the type field this version now emits, and an older apm
    cannot accept the --type=expert this plugin sends.

 -- liangjunzhao <junzhao.liang@spacemit.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 17:20:00 +0800


